00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:02.629 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  monday september 12th i'm julie chang  for the wall street journal filling in  for zoe thomas  the people who swap stocks on robin hood  aren't always the same as traditional  traders they're younger for one robin  hood says the average age of its users  is 32 and they aren't necessarily  interested in the same stocks as the  pros on wall street  but there's more than 22 million of them  according to the company  and their trades move markets and now  robin hood has created its own index  showing the stocks its users are most  excited about  among them tesla apple amazon and ford  meme stocks like amc and gamestop and  the chinese electric car company neo so  what else can this new index tell us  about how people are using robinhood  here to explain is wsj markets reporter  alexander ossipovic alex thank you so  much for coming on the show thanks for  having me  so can you start us off by telling us  more about this new index what exactly  has robin hood created here so robin  hood has created something that it is  calling the robinhood investor index  it is an index like the dow jones  industrial average or the s p 500  but instead of trying to track a broad  swath of stocks in the stock market it  will track the top 100 stocks that are  popular among robin hood investors  the way that they're constructing this  is that they're looking across their  user base which involves millions of  users  and identifying the stocks in which it  says that customers have the most  conviction what that means is how much  of your portfolio percentage-wise have  you allocated to a particular stock so  if you put 50 of your stock in gamestop  and a lot of other people have done the  same thing gamestop will rate highly in  this index uh it's a bit different from  the way some other indexes are  constructed and it is notable because  many consumers on robinhood don't  actually have that much money in their  accounts they might have as little as 20  worth of stocks in their account and  their commitment to a particular stock  would still be noted  by this index one other thing that's  important to note about the robinhood  investor index is that it's not tradable  it's not something you can go buy or you  know go short and bet against and robin  hood has told me that they don't plan to  license the index to etf issuers if they  did then one day you would probably be  able to buy a robin hood index etf they  say at this point in time anyway there's  no such plan  why did robin hood say that it was  creating this new index  so robin hood gave two reasons for this  so first of all  they said that their customers like to  know what fellow investors are doing  the other reason is that robinhood is  basically trying to  reshape the pr narrative around the  company and the sort of people who trade  on robin hood  robin hood investors have a reputation  for  piling into kind of volatile risky  stocks and losing a lot of money doing  it and robin hood says that they see  their own data and they think that  that's a false narrative and what  they've decided to do is put out this  index which will show generally speaking  how well robin hood investors are doing  and if anybody asks the question again  about how are they doing you'll just be  able to point to the index  you know in 2018 robin hood released  hourly data on stocks and the stock's  popularity among its customers but then  it shut off that data in the summer of  2020 saying it was worried that that  data could be misconstrued or  misunderstood isn't this essentially the  same thing but instead of hourly updates  they're going to give us monthly updates  so the data you're referring to was  popularized by a website called robin  track that basically gathered it all up  and published it in a fairly easy to use  interface and became fairly popular for  a while in 2020  the original data that robin hood put  out  was every single hour you would get the  actual  total holdings of uh various stocks on  robin hood  and you'd actually see like you know how  many shares were being amassed by the  robin hood user base from that you could  figure out if they're buying or selling  a particular stocks  there were some concerns at the time  that this data was so detailed that  would actually allow  hedge funds to kind of game what was  going on so if they saw that a lot of  retail investors were piling into a  particular stock they could either try  to catch the wave and buy the stock too  or decide that all those retail  investors were dumb and but against them  once this started getting a lot of  attention  robin hood abruptly cut off the data  feed that was providing this  the robin track website still exists but  it basically hasn't been updated since  august 2020 when robin hood cut off the  data i would say this index is fairly  different because the composition is  being only released once a month and  it's kind of somewhat backward looking  you won't have as much of a real-time  sense of what's going on  in addition to that you don't have quite  the same level of granularity about you  know how many shares of stock are held  okay so then big picture you know you've  been speaking with market watchers what  have their reactions to this been so far  do they think it's going to be useful or  not  so i have spoken to a few people about  this and i think that there is some  consensus that it would be it'll be  interesting data because in general  since we've had this force of small  investors coming out and becoming kind  of more influential and you know  swinging the market around there's been  more interest in having metrics to  follow what they're doing how do we get  data on what they're buying what they're  interested what the holdings are and  this will be a picture that will be kind  of a glimpse into that world  provided by robin hood which is one of  the biggest names in this particular  space  that said i wouldn't make an overly big  deal out of it in particular because  there are no plans as yet to launch an  etf based on the data or a fund based on  the data if it were a tradable index  something that people could actually  sort of place bets on then that could  lead to some interesting effects as  you'd see you know hedge funds literally  betting against robin hood investors by  shorting the index uh it doesn't look  like that's going to happen anytime soon  all right that was our reporter  alexander ossopovich alex thank you so  much for breaking this down for us  thanks julie great talking to you  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing for more tech stories check out  our website wsj.com i'm julie chang for  the wall street journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

